From 6d68212be1b5238a09e6a2284b116d1b26675801 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Somalapuram Amaranath <somalapuram@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 08:51:41 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] minigbm: drop DRV_PC_FORCE_LINEAR now that a real GPU renders

Patch 0001 added the linear-only pc platform because SwiftShader rendered
on the CPU and i915_bo_map() only takes the mmap path when
tiling == I915_TILING_NONE, so a tiled buffer could not be locked at all.
Its own comment set the condition for removing it: once Mesa is shipping
and the GPU does the rendering. That is now true on iris, radeonsi and
zink-on-NVK.

Keeping it cost real performance. It pinned modifier.order to
{ DRM_FORMAT_MOD_LINEAR } and made i915_add_combinations() return before
any X-tiled or Y/4-tiled combination was registered, so every buffer was
untiled including SurfaceFlinger's client target. Under PRIME offload the
dGPU then renders untiled and the iGPU scans out untiled every frame.
Measured with it on, SuperTuxKart on zink/NVK: 321 frames in 30.7s
(10.4 fps), 592 missed, 27.5 ms average frame duration.

Safe by construction: the combinations below the old early-return add
tiled variants only for usages carrying neither an SW_ nor a LINEAR flag,
so anything needing CPU access keeps a linear combination.

Change-Id: I4d7353769e16e5129037a035839fa7c04e22727f
---
 Android.bp | 29 ++++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Android.bp b/Android.bp
index 1929cff..fcc2fdb 100644
--- a/Android.bp
+++ b/Android.bp
@@ -75,13 +75,32 @@ intel_cflags = [
     "-DDRV_I915",
     "-DDRV_XE",
 ]
-// Bare-metal x86_64 PC. Intel backends plus a linear-only allocation policy,
-// because this configuration renders with SwiftShader on the CPU and tiled
-// buffers cannot be CPU-mapped. Drop DRV_PC_FORCE_LINEAR once Mesa (iris/anv)
-// is shipping and the GPU does the rendering.
+// Bare-metal x86_64 PC. Intel backends.
+//
+// DRV_PC_FORCE_LINEAR is GONE, on the condition its own comment set: it existed
+// because SwiftShader rendered on the CPU and i915_bo_map() only takes the mmap
+// path when tiling == I915_TILING_NONE, so a tiled buffer could not be locked at
+// all. Mesa now drives the GPU on all three vendors -- iris, radeonsi and zink
+// on NVK -- so nothing renders on the CPU any more.
+//
+// Keeping it was costing real performance. It pinned modifier.order to
+// { DRM_FORMAT_MOD_LINEAR } and made i915_add_combinations() return before any
+// X-tiled or Y/4-tiled combination was registered, so EVERY buffer was untiled,
+// including SurfaceFlinger's client target. Under PRIME render offload that
+// means the dGPU renders untiled and the iGPU scans out untiled every frame.
+// Measured with it on: SuperTuxKart at 10.4 fps (321 frames in 30.7s, 592
+// missed, 27.5 ms/frame) with 100% client composition.
+//
+// Dropping it is safe by construction rather than by hope: the combinations
+// below the old early-return add tiled variants only for usages that carry
+// NEITHER an SW_ nor a LINEAR flag (see render_not_linear / unset_flags(...,
+// linear_mask) in i915.c), so anything that still needs CPU access keeps a
+// linear combination. The risk that remains is the cross-device one -- NVK and
+// i915 must agree on a modifier for a PRIME-shared buffer. If they cannot, the
+// symptom is corruption or a failed import, not a slow path, and this flag is
+// the single thing to put back.
 pc_cflags = intel_cflags + [
     "-DHAS_DMABUF_SYSTEM_HEAP",
-    "-DDRV_PC_FORCE_LINEAR",
 ]
 
 meson_cflags = ["-DDRV_MESON"]
